{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 96 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8a0050979c1e632a7e93ab79c3eb3eb703a492bfcee2996626d74805a59389bc" :edb/db-before-t 95 :edb/db-after-t 96 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533408 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:45:09.670Z" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186054676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290509 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186054851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290448 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186054851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290449 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052201 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186055147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289749 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055147 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=2x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055147 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $x\\ln y = 2x$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186055147 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ [(x)(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))] & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052239 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186055155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290154 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055155 1071 "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n)\\,,\\,f(1)=-2\\,,\\,f(2)=-1,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe third term of the sequence is{{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186055155 1071 "Consider the following sequence:\n$f(n + 2) = f(n + 1) + f(n), f(1) =-2, f(2) =-1, n \\ge 1$\nThe third term of the sequence is {{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186055155 1074 "The third term is represented by $f(3).$\n\nTo compute $f(3),$ we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & =(-1)+(-2) \\\\ f(3) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third term of the sequence is $f(3)=-3.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290467 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290513 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059657 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290551 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290291 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290292 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290293 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290294 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290295 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290296 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290566 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290565 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290568 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059678 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290583 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059679 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290564 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290582 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290450 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290451 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290576 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290579 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290577 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290580 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186059850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290461 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057968 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290098 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060226 1071 "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}?$\n\n- $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$\n- $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$\n- $-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060226 1071 "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $θ =-\\frac{2π}{3}$?\n\n1. $\\frac{4π}{3}$\n2. $\\frac{10π}{3}$\n3. $-\\frac{8π}{3}$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060226 1074 "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}.$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$:\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\,✓$\n\n- $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-\\frac{14\\pi }{3}$\n\nOf the given angles, all three are coterminal with $-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nSo the correct answer is I, II, and III." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058082 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290138 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060245 1071 "Find the exact value of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060245 1071 "Find the exact value of $4\\sin (7.5^{∘})\\cos (7.5^{∘})\\cos (15^{∘})$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060245 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={7.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({7.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {7.5}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({7.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({15}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}) & =2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({15}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x={15}^{∘},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{15}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{15}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {15}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({7.5}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({15}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058094 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289818 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060247 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060247 1071 "What is $(\\sin (\\frac{π}{8}))/(\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}))$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060247 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}}{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058113 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289871 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060251 1071 "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060251 1071 "What is $\\arctan (\\sqrt{3})$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060251 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by:\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058125 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289744 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060253 1071 "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060253 1071 "What is $\\arcsin (-\\frac{1}{2})$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060253 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058137 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290039 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060255 1071 "Find the exact value of $(\\sin (\\frac{π}{12}) - \\cos (\\frac{π}{12}))^{2}$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060255 1071 "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))}^{2}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060255 1074 "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})]-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =1-(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}))}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058143 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289919 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060256 1071 "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186060256 1071 "What is $\\arccos (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186060256 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290453 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290454 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064165 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290465 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290433 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290263 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290439 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290264 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290315 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064171 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290316 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064171 1038 #edb/ref 1759218629035