38 #edb/ref 17592186290387 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290388 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290432 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290434 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290591 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290443 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290444 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290342 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290346 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290592 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290605 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290597 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290606 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064251 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290537 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290468 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064385 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290452 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061658 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289899 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064588 1071 "For the function $f(x) = 2x^{3} - 6x + 12$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x =-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n2. $f^{″}(-1) < 0$\n3. $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x =-1$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064588 1071 "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-1$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064588 1074 "We need to identify whether $x=-1$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6=6{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ 6({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 6(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-1)=-12<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-1$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=-1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061667 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290186 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064592 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{5} + 5x^{3} - 90x + 108$. Given that $f^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) < 0$\n2. $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x =-\\sqrt{3}$\n3. $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x =-\\sqrt{3}$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064592 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064592 1074 "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061776 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289902 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064624 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{3} + 3$ and the point $P(2, 11)$ that lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, calculate $(f^{-1})^{′}(11)$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064624 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3$ and the point $P(2,11)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064624 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,11).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,11)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(11,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(11))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061812 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289783 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064630 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n$f(x) = x^{2}$ on the interval $[- 8, 6]$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064630 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)={x}^{2}$ on the interval $[-8,6].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064630 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-8,6]$ and differentiable on $(-8,6).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-8,6)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-8$ and $b=6$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(6)-f(-8)}{6-(-8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}-(-8)^{2}}{14} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =-2 \\\\ c & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061818 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289789 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064631 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n$f(x) =-3\\sqrt{x} + 2$ on the interval $[0, 2]$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064631 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)=-3\\sqrt{x}+2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064631 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\sqrt{2}+2)-(-3\\sqrt{0}+2)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\sqrt{x}+2)=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ c & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061849 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290115 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064637 1071 "A pumping station adds sand to Sandy Beach at a rate of $3\\sqrt{t}$ cubic yards per hour. The tide removes the sand from the beach at a rate of $2t$ cubic yards per hour. If at time $t = 0$ the beach contains $1500$ cubic yards of sand, write an expression for $A(t)$, the total number of cubic yards of sand on the beach at any time $t > 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064637 1071 "A pumping station adds sand to Sandy Beach at a rate of $3\\sqrt{t}$ cubic yards per hour. The tide removes the sand from the beach at a rate of $2t$ cubic yards per hour. If at time $t=0$ the beach contains $1\\,500$ cubic yards of sand, write an expression for $A(t),$ the total number of cubic yards of sand on the beach at any time $t>0.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064637 1074 "Let $a(t)=3\\sqrt{t}-2t$ be the rate at which the volume of sand at Sandy Beach is changing. Note that we subtract $2t$ to indicate the rate at which the volume is decreasing.\n\nLet $A(T)$ be the volume of sand on the beach at any time after $T$ minutes. Using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1\\,500+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3\\sqrt{t}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1\\,500+{(2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =1\\,500+2{T}^{3/2}-{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have \n\n$$\nA(t)=2{t}^{3/2}-{t}^{2}+1\\,500.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061897 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289829 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064645 1071 "For the function $f(x) = 6x^{2} - 4x^{3}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{″}(0) > 0$\n2. $x = 0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n3. $f^{″}(1) < 0$\n4. $x = 1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064645 1071 "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064645 1074 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III and VI are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061939 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289768 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064652 1071 "$\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064652 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{4}{x^{2} + 1}dx$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064652 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061945 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290062 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064653 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186064653 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - 4x^{2}}}dx$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186064653 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290310 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068691 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290311 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290309 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290312 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068692 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290313 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290524 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290510 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290520 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068867 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290366 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290593 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290594 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290595 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068879 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290596 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290469 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290598 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290599 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290600 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290601 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290602 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290603 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186068922 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290372 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066167 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289929 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069146 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int3x^{2}(x^{3} + 4)^{5}dx$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069146 1071 "Calculate $\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069146 1074 "We note that $3{x}^{2}$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{3}+4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{3}+4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 3{x}^{2}({x}^{3}+4)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{3}+4)^{6}}{6}+C.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066173 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290024 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069147 1071 "$\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069147 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int2x(x^{2} + 1)^{12}dx$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069147 1074 "We note that $2x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the parentheses. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\int {u}^{12}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{{u}^{13}}{13}+C\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 2x({x}^{2}+1)^{12}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{13}}{13}+C.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066261 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290162 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069177 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-x^{2} - 12x - 35}}dx$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069177 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069177 1074 "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-12x-35 & =-({x}^{2}+12x+35) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+[{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}]+35) \\\\  & =-((x+6)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-13/2$ | $-11/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-12x-35}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-13/2}^{-11/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+6)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066279 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289828 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069180 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{3x^{2}}{\\sqrt{x^{3} + 7}}dx$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069180 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069180 1074 "Let $u={x}^{3}+7.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066297 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290005 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069183 1071 "![](images/09/098189dc8e96b01cb045a198eef3f00ba2df6594fc14675cad81246fc2d25964.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x + 2)(x - 3) \\ge 0$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186069183 1071 "![](images/09/098189dc8e96b01cb045a198eef3f00ba2df6594fc14675cad81246fc2d25964.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x+2)(x-3)\\ge 0.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186069183 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-3)\\ge 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-3)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3e/3e80a6e08ccff87314e5081a08332f0de508f6db02446742d1c93416d41a4b3c.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 3.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186073720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290354 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186073720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290355 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186073720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290356 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290554 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290555 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078421 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290363 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290607 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290608 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078555 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290462 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078555 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290609 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290610 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290611 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290612 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290613 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075752 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290048 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078809 1071 "If the sum of the first $50$ terms of an arithmetic series is $S_{50} = 600$, and the last term is $a_{50} = 21.8$, then the first term of the series is $a_{1} =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078809 1071 "If the sum of the first $50$ terms of an arithmetic series is ${S}_{50}=600,$ and the last term is ${a}_{50}=21.8,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078809 1074 "We are given the last term of our series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{50}=21.8$, $N=50$, and ${S}_{N}=600$ into the formula above and solving for ${a}_{1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600 & =\\frac{50}{2}({a}_{1}+21.8) \\\\ 600 & =25({a}_{1}+21.8) \\\\ \\frac{600}{25} & ={a}_{1}+21.8 \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}+21.8 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075753 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290049 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078810 1071 "If the $15$th term of an arithmetic series is $-53$ and the sum of the first $21$ terms is $-777$, then the first term of the series is $a_{1} =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078810 1071 "If the $15$th term of an arithmetic series is $-53$ and the sum of the first $21$ terms is $-777,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078810 1074 "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=21$ and ${S}_{N}=-777$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -777 & =\\frac{21}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(21-1)d) \\\\ -1\\,554= & 21(2{a}_{1}+20d) \\\\ -74 & =2{a}_{1}+20d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $15$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{15} & ={a}_{1}+(15-1)d \\\\ -53 & ={a}_{1}+14d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-53={a}_{1}+14d \\\\ -74=2{a}_{1}+20d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}106 & =-2{a}_{1}-28d \\\\ -74 & =2{a}_{1}+20d \\\\ 32 & =-8d \\\\ d & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=-4$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-53 & ={a}_{1}+14d \\\\ -53 & ={a}_{1}+14(-4) \\\\ -53 & ={a}_{1}-56 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075806 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289819 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078828 1071 "The volume $V$, in gallons, of water in a pool $t$ minutes after the pool starts filling can be modeled by the function $V(t) = 5\\sqrt{t} + 2$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t = 4$ minutes?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078828 1071 "The volume $V$, in gallons, of water in a pool $t$ minutes after the pool starts filling can be modeled by the function $V(t)=5\\sqrt{t}+2.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078828 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is equal to ${V}^{′}(4).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\sqrt{t}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{\\frac{1}{2}}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the water volume when $t=4$ minutes is $\\frac{5}{4}$ gallons per minute." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075821 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290076 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078838 1071 "If the sum of the first $20$ terms of an arithmetic series is $S_{20} = 301$, and the common difference is $d = 1.5$, then the first term of the series is $a_{1} =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186078838 1071 "If the sum of the first $20$ terms of an arithmetic series is ${S}_{20}=301,$ and the common difference is $d=1.5,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186078838 1074 "We are given the common difference of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=20$, $d=1.5$, and ${S}_{N}=301$ into the formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}301 & =\\frac{20}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(20-1)(1.5)) \\\\ 301 & =10(2{a}_{1}+28.5) \\\\ 30.1 & =2{a}_{1}+28.5 \\\\ 1.6 & =2{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290266 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290280 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290384 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080735 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290199 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083617 1071 "For the parametric curve\n$x = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}, y = \\frac{e^{t}}{2t}, - ∞ < t < ∞$,\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = 5$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083617 1071 "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=5.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083617 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}}{4{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-4{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=5,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}(5-1)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}\\cdot 4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080759 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290118 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083621 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083621 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = e^{2t}, y = t^{2}, 0 \\le t \\le 2$.\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083621 1074 "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080766 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290008 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083623 1071 "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = 2x^{3} - 15x^{2} + 24x + 9$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083623 1071 "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+9.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083623 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+24x+9) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-30x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-30x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186080782 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289912 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083632 1071 "An object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9\\,\\text{km/min}.$ It takes the object $4\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe height of the building is{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186083632 1071 "An object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9km/min$. It takes the object $4s$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*\n\nThe height of the building is {{field-1}} $m$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186083632 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The object falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(15)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =60+(9.8)(8) \\\\  & =138.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $138.4\\,\\text{m}$ high." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290556 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290552 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087696 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290447 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290604 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290587 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290588 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290529 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290589 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087865 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290590 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290471 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186087895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290472 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085159 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085160 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290134 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290252 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088084 1071 "Solve the equation $x^{2} - 2x =-50$.$x =$\n{{field-1}}\n$±$\n{{field-2}} $\\textrm{i}$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088084 1071 "Solve the equation ${x}^{2}-2x=-50.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088084 1074 "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+50=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=-2,$ and $c=50.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(50)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-200}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-196}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 14\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 7\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=1+7\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=1-7\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085207 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290133 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088095 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t) = 2t^{3} - 3t^{2} + 2t$, where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t = 2$ seconds." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088095 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t$, where $t$ is measured in seconds.  Find the particle's acceleration at $t=2$ seconds." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088095 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-6t+2) \\\\  & =12t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=2$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =12(2)-6 \\\\  & =24-6 \\\\  & =18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=2$ seconds is $18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085219 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289773 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088097 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t) = 4t^{3} + 10t^{2} - 15$, where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the particle's acceleration at $t = 3$ seconds." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088097 1071 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}-15$, where $t$ is measured in seconds.  Find the particle's acceleration at $t=3$ seconds." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088097 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}+10{t}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}+20t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}+20t) \\\\  & =24t+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=3$, we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(3) & =24(3)+20 \\\\  & =72+20 \\\\  & =92\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration when $t=3$ seconds is $92\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085230 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289992 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088100 1071 "Express $\\cos^{2} (\\frac{3π}{4}) - \\sin^{2} (\\frac{3π}{4})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088100 1071 "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088100 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085236 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289978 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088101 1071 "Express $2\\cos^{2} (\\frac{5π}{3}) - 1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186088101 1071 "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088101 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186088102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085242 