$3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $B$ and $3$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{3\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{A}_{3\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $A$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{✓}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 1}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099234 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186101327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289808 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101327 1071 "Let\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat are the dimensions of $BA$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186101327 1071 "Let\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].$\nWhat are the dimensions of $BA?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101327 1074 "We notice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $BA$ has dimensions $2\\times 2:$\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{✓}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}={BA}_{2\\times 2}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099260 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186101333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290236 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101333 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(5 + x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.97)^{7}$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$(4.97)^{7} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186101333 1071 "Use the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(5+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(4.97)^{7}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$(4.97)^{7}≈$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101333 1074 "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=5,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5+x)}^{7} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0}){(5)}^{7}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1}){(5)}^{7-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2}){(5)}^{7-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(5)}^{7}+7{(5)}^{6}{(x)}^{1}+21{(5)}^{5}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =78\\,125+7(15\\,625)(x)+21(3\\,125)({x}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =78\\,125+109\\,375x+65\\,625{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(5+x)}^{7}≈78\\,125+109\\,375x+65\\,625{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the above approximation of $(5+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4.97)^{7} & =(5+(-0.03))^{7} \\\\  & ≈78\\,125+109\\,375(-0.03)+65\\,625(-0.03)^{2} \\\\  & =78\\,125+109\\,375(-0.03)+65\\,625(0.000\\,9) \\\\  & =78\\,125-3\\,281.25+59.062\\,5 \\\\  & ≈74\\,902.81,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099286 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186101342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290197 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101342 1071 "Find all values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ on the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186101342 1071 "Find all values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x) = 3x^{2}$ on the interval $[- 2, 2]$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186101342 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ({x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[{2}^{3}-(-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =4 \\\\ 3{c}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ c & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290298 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290299 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290300 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290301 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290302 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290303 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105715 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290307 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290308 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290417 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290421 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290442 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290445 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290267 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290268 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290277 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290279 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290275 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290276 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290409 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290492 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290493 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290494 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290495 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290496 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290497 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290498 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186105924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290499 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102818 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289946 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106162 1071 "![](images/5c/5c0196b13b3f20ba05313a9604e73b13251937b525a12fd5b9997e99f4ae21a0.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106162 1071 "![](images/5c/5c0196b13b3f20ba05313a9604e73b13251937b525a12fd5b9997e99f4ae21a0.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106162 1074 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(-1,1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/2f/2f8d6ec6c9a71e61180ad04c25d623c40dadfdd623e1e2eaa8458915bcaca2f8.png)" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102824 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290257 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106163 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1), (0,-1)$, and $(0, 0)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T$?\n\n1. $(4,-1)$\n2. $(0, 0)$\n3. $(-4,-8)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106163 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,-1),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(-4,-8)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106163 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-8),$ $(-1,-2)$, and $(0,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102914 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290111 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106178 1071 "Let $a = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$. Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106178 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106178 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot x \\\\  & =x-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ x-20 & =0 \\\\ x & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186103016 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289886 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106195 1071 "Given the function $f(x) =-x^{4} + 2x^{3} + 6$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x = 0$ is a local maximum\n2. $x = 0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n3. $x = \\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106195 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106195 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}=-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-4(-1)^{2}(-1-\\frac{3}{2})=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{2}(1-\\frac{3}{2})=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-4(2)^{2}(2-\\frac{3}{2})=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186103064 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290116 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106203 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3, 1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v = ⟨2, 1, 3⟩$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP = \\sqrt{14}$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106203 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106203 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩)-⟨3,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩+1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨5,2,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩-1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186103125 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289753 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106214 1071 "For the function $f(x) = x - 2\\sin x$ defined for $x ∈ (0, 2π)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x = \\frac{π}{3}$ is a local minimum\n2. $x = \\frac{π}{3}$ is a local maximum\n3. $x = \\frac{5π}{3}$ is a local maximum\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106214 1071 "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106214 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186103155 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186106219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289842 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186106219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 131