 are $n=10$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=-110,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-110) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=1\\,350,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 1350 \\\\  & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =135-{(-11)}^{2} \\\\  & =135-121 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186115576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186112392 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186115576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289840 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186115576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186115576 1071 "Compute the standard deviation $\\sigma $ of the following data set:\n\n$$\n-9,\\,-7,\\,1,\\,3,\\,4\n$$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186115576 1071 "Compute the standard deviation $σ$ of the following data set:\n$-9,-7, 1, 3, 4$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n$σ =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186115576 1074 "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-9-7+1+3+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [(-9)^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [81+49+1+9+16] \\\\  & =\\frac{156}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{156}{5}-{(-\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{156}{5}-\\frac{64}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{780-64}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{716}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sigma  & =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{716}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{179}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186119792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290404 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186119792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290405 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186119792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290406 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186119792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290407 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186119792 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290408 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186120170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117177 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186120170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117178 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186120170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117179 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186120170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290139 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186120170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186120170 1071 "Given  the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=⟨\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$\n{{field-1}}$,0\\,⟩$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186120170 1071 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨3\\cos t, 3\\sin t, 4t⟩$, find the principal normal vector $N(t)$ at an arbitrary point on the curve.$N(t) =$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}}\n$$,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186120170 1074 "First, we find the unit tangent vector $T(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{-\\frac{3}{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124504 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290401 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290403 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124506 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290402 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124506 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290410 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290488 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290489 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290490 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124507 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290491 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121673 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186124851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290108 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124851 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the straight line that passes through $P(5,-10, 2)$ and is parallel to $v = ⟨ - 6,-2, 1⟩$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186124851 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the straight line that passes through $P(5,-10,2)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-6,-2,1⟩.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124851 1074 "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 