at describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-5,7,1)$ and $Q(-4,-1,6).$\n\n| $x=$ | {{field-1}} | $t\\in [$ {{field-2}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | $7-8t$ |  |\n| $z=$ | $1+5t$ |  |" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186124853 1074 "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -8t \\\\ 5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5+t \\\\ 7-8t \\\\ 1+5t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-5+t \\\\ y=7-8t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290456 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129087 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290531 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129087 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290533 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290457 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290532 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290480 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290481 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290482 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129129 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290483 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126842 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186129565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290222 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129565 1071 "The point $P$ is located in the third quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{2}{5}$. What is the $y$-coordinate of $P$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186129565 1071 "The point $P$ is located in the third quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{2}{5}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $P?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129565 1074 "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\frac{4}{25})+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $y$ is negative in the third quadrant. So our final answer is $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126855 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186129568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290127 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129568 1071 "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $9$." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186129568 1071 "Find the equation of the midline for a transformed cosine function with a minimum value of $-5$ and a maximum value of $9.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129568 1074 "A sinusoidal graph's midline (or vertical shift) is the horizontal line that divides it vertically in half. \n\nThe equation of the midline is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{\\text{min}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{max}}$ are the minimum and maximum values of the function. The midline equals the average of the maximum and minimum values. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{\\text{min}}+{y}_{\\text{max}}}{2} & =\\frac{-5+9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midline is $y=2.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126867 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186129570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290239 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129570 1071 "![](images/c2/c2644fc906d4588bc39aa92d887836ff8942f5eb4b28de1baf3dc1030d2dca67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin θ = \\frac{1}{3}$, what is the value of $\\cos θ$?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186129570 1071 "![](images/c2/c2644fc906d4588bc39aa92d887836ff8942f5eb4b28de1baf3dc1030d2dca67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3},$ what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186129570 1074 "![](images/49/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152122 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290389 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152122 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290390 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152122 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290391 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290359 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290392 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290393 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152123 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290394 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290395 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290396 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152124 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290397 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152125 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290398 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152125 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290399 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152125 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290400 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149602 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186152518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290144 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152518 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $4u^{3}(v - 2)^{2}$ and $5u(v - 2)^{5}$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186152518 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $4{u}^{3}(v-2)^{2}$ and $5u(v-2)^{5}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186152518 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(4,5):$\n\n- Multiples of $4:$ $\\,4,\\,8,\\,12,\\,16,\\,20,\\,24,…$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,\\,25,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(4,5)=20.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of ${u}^{3}(v-2)^{2}$ and $u(v-2)^{5},$ we find the largest powers for $u$ and $(v-2)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For ${u}^{3}$ and $u,$ the largest power is $3.$\n\n- For $(v-2)^{2}$ and $(v-2)^{5},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\nNow, we raise $u$ and $(v-2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({u}^{3}(v-2)^{2},\\,u(v-2)^{5})={u}^{3}\\cdot (v-2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(4{u}^{3}(v-2)^{2},\\,5u(v-2)^{5})=20{u}^{3}(v-2)^{5}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290418 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290419 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290420 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290422 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290423 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290424 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290425 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290426 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290427 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290428 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290429 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290430 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290431 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290511 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154600 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186157690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186154601 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186157690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290119 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290211 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157690 1071 "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $x^{2} \\cdot 4^{x + 1} + 5x \\cdot 4^{x + 1} + 6 \\cdot 4^{x + 1} = 0$ are$x =$\n{{field-1}} and $x =$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186157690 1071 "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+5x\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {4}^{x+1}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186157690 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+5x\\cdot {4}^{x+1}+6\\cdot {4}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x+6){4}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+5x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{4}^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x+3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,-2.$\n\n- The equation ${4}^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because ${4}^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-2.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290347 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290348 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290349 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290350 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290351 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290352 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186171921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290353 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186172432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169704 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186172432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290170 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186172432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186172432 1074 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/ff/ff5d1682ca8d142ad4cf73b9a5c996164653d940157efd695d1da13d34900404.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=4.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{4}={e}^{(4)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186172433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186169710 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186172433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289904 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186172433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186172433 1074 "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $5$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $5$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{5}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/5}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/53/53c8822760ceb51c1d1bd685947dbfefa7dfdc3490027db62cbcaf5d9aa24335.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=2.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{2}={e}^{2(2)\\pi \\text{i}/5}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290500 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290502 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290503 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290322 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290466 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290501 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290504 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290505 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290476 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290506 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290507 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175930 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290508 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175933 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290534 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290535 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186175958 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290536 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186176439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174784 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186176439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290117 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186176439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186176439 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t) = 6t^{2} - 2t$. What is the total displacement of the particle between $t = 1$ and $t = 4$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186176439 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=1$ and $t=4?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186176439 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[2(4)^{3}-(4)^{2}]-[2(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =[128-16]-[2-1] \\\\  & =112-1 \\\\  & =111.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=1$ and $t=4$ is $111.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290435 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290436 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290437 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290438 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290440 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179653 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290441 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290538 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290550 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290540 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290541 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290542 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290543 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290544 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290458 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290545 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290546 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290547 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290548 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290549 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290561 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184157 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290446 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184174 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290382 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184174 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290411 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184174 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290412 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184174 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290413 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290414 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184176 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290415 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184198 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290367 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184217 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290361 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184217 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290362 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184218 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290365 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290364 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290614 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290463 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290615 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290616 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290617 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290618 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290629 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290630 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290631 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290632 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290515 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290297 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181836 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186184609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289772 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184609 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{2} - 2e^{x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is concave up on $(- ∞, 0)$\n2. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(- ∞, 0)$\n3. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0, ∞)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186184609 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184609 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181863 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186184613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289982 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184613 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x) = 2x - \\ln (1 + x)$ is concave up." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186184613 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is concave up." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184613 1074 "First, we notice that the domain of the function is $x\\in (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nA function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2-\\frac{1}{1+x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}>0\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\ne -1$ the second derivative is always positive since both the numerator and denominator are positive. Therefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is concave up on its entire domain, $(-1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181869 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186184614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289923 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184614 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{x}\\sin x$, defined on $x ∈ (0, π)$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0, \\frac{π}{2})$\n2. $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{π}{2}, π)$\n3. $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{π}{2}, π)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186184614 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186184614 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](images/41/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186188227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290470 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186188227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290512 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186188228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290519 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186198253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290288 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186198253 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290289 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186198254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290290 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186198451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290571 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186203904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290345 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290484 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186204373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186201277 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186204373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290234 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186204373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186204373 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = 6\\sin (5x + 40^{∘})$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186204373 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+{40}^{∘}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186204373 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=5x+{40}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 5x+{40}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 5x & ={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x={10}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186233630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289955 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186233630 1074 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{'}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/ec/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 false] [17592186233630 1074 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6x(x-1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=6x(x-1)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=1,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/ec/ec7ea6baa82d60a6cf472c904460190042d889d86cb092b19d9809111bd93b36.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186233659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290070 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288031 1081 "y-5" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288031 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288031 1084 #uuid "000ea0dc-e83d-59b3-891d-03ba54bde6ba" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288032 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288032 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288032 1084 #uuid "0043b9dd-acd7-5722-ae3f-3f717ea8f717" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288033 1081 "74902.81" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288033 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288033 1084 #uuid "0083447b-7bf3-52b5-bfed-3ab32b17af3c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288034 1081 "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}(9)}{1+\\operatorname{ln}(9)}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288034 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288034 1084 #uuid "00837155-5f27-503a-a52f-c2a043e66010" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288035 1081 "\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{2}}}{2}+C" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288035 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288035 1084 #uuid "00acbd21-382b-5ab9-ae57-615f612cac26" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186288036 1081 "An arc of a