ue] [17592186290260 1080 #uuid "ff5c4d7e-c117-5b76-9e60-4bd62906faaa" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289207 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290261 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290261 1077 "field-2" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290261 1079 #edb/ref 17592186289207 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290261 1080 #uuid "ffba8f81-6bad-5095-8d2a-7a84517eadec" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288383 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288979 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289180 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289276 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289626 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1077 "selection" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1079 #edb/ref 17592186288383 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290262 1080 #uuid "fff57b46-70ef-54bd-87fb-a12c9a20446e" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289953 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290263 1069 "q-20979" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290263 1071 "For the function $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ on the interval $[-1,2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global maximum value is $13$\n- The global minimum value is $-7$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290263 1072 #uuid "89e4e3cf-d4a8-5dba-b035-5946e9d4ce09" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290263 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=13-9{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-18x+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =6x(x-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n6x(x-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,3.\n$$\n\nSince $x=3$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =13-9(-1)^{2}+2(-1)^{3}=2 \\\\ f(0) & =13-9(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}=13 \\\\ f(2) & =13-9(2)^{2}+2(2)^{3}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=13,$ so the global maximum is $13.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=-7,$ so the global minimum is $-7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](images/1a/1a9d2f0db241c0a209c315a8e901e40a44258f339bbaa7bee36198b58fe2684d.png)" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290250 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290264 1069 "q-20982" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290264 1071 "For the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290264 1072 #uuid "1676c625-f838-58d0-a1ed-b9b72fc63c79" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290264 1074 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3}+4x & =0 \\\\ -4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -4x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-8<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =4>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement IV is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289998 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290265 1069 "q-20983" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290265 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=2$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290265 1072 #uuid "206675d2-b06e-5deb-b30d-ae541d334107" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290265 1074 "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-24x+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x-24.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =12\\cdot 2-24=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =6(1)^{2}-24(1)+24=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =6(3)^{2}-24(3)+24=6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=2$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290225 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290266 1069 "q-20988" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290266 1071 "![](images/0b/0b77bdde611689095cdea6a3a08ce9828b9e20040283dc0c251250538a5cfba3.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2}-2t,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290266 1072 #uuid "8f7deccd-b00e-58b4-a06a-44a78bd3e26d" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290266 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6t-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{6t-2}=\\frac{t}{3t-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289897 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290267 1069 "q-21017" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290267 1071 "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=5-7x-4{x}^{2}$ is strictly decreasing." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290267 1072 #uuid "7346093a-8a98-59c3-8b2f-e823ce28a996" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290267 1074 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-7-8x \\\\  & =-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-8(x+\\frac{7}{8})<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) & <0 \\\\ 0 & <8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) \\\\ 0 & <x+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{8} & <x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty ).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290124 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290268 1069 "q-21019" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290268 1071 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{x}$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290268 1072 #uuid "0e4d9268-7a0b-5c23-a4b9-30e5cb46a1a5" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290268 1074 "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}>0.\n$$\n\nUsing that $x>0,$ the above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-1 & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x+1)(x-1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors ${x}^{2}+1$ and $x+1$ are positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290012 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290269 1069 "q-21034" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290269 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x-12.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290269 1072 #uuid "27d49068-bf4c-5b09-aaea-4b26bbdb5153" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290269 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+6=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =6(0)+6=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289767 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290270 1069 "q-21035" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290270 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290270 1072 #uuid "f17a5b4b-0b74-571a-8e3d-75e6d31adccd" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290270 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290091 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290271 1069 "q-21041" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290271 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290271 1072 #uuid "50e30d5b-7fd0-5781-9317-bd75d6faf156" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290271 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(1-\\frac{3}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=3.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=2$ and $x=4$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(2)^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =\\frac{2}{(4)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(4)^{4}}=\\frac{1}{128}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289983 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290272 1069 "q-21044" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290272 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=6{x}^{3}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290272 1072 #uuid "72bd9c1b-8a89-551b-9ae2-b4bbe161eca6" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290272 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =18{x}^{2}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36x+\\frac{36}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 36(\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{{x}^{4}}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{5}+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =36(-2)+\\frac{36}{(-2)^{4}}=-\\frac{279}{4}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)+\\frac{36}{(1)^{4}}=72>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289796 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290273 1069 "q-21045" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290273 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-3x+7.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290273 1072 #uuid "59dfd8c2-6314-5f98-a642-db692ff36a00" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290273 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.  \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n2x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=2$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-2=-2<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =2(2)-2=2>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290089 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290274 1069 "q-21047" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290274 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=8{x}^{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{x}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290274 1072 #uuid "47254316-6f9b-52c5-92f7-d2e76625d399" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290274 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =16x-3+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{4}) & =16-\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}=-112<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =16-\\frac{2}{(1)^{3}}=14>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290064 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290275 1069 "q-21063" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290275 1071 "![](images/65/6501b1a7e9429c786905c11835410111c32ac0337f1ba63bff130d327350316c.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290275 1072 #uuid "dcfd5567-90cc-5d33-9aaa-36581e272ed2" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290275 1074 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(6,3)$, $(8,4)$ and $(10,5).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/a9/a91af151d3dc0e48aef0eef58012f6c9f72aaff02aafae2d55fe0714c29c96e3.png)" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290059 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290276 1069 "q-21065" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290276 1071 "![](images/db/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290276 1072 #uuid "c723b05a-0b3d-5522-bbda-5b3f8ac9a3ef" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290276 1074 "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/2e/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289827 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290277 1069 "q-21066" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290277 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,2)$, $(-1,1)$ and $(3,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(12,6)$\n- $(4,2)$\n- $(4,6)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290277 1072 #uuid "b1e5b57c-3102-59cd-a153-f7966b8c71f1" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290277 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(12,6)$, $(4,2)$ and $(12,6).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289876 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290278 1069 "q-210662" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290278 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2.7<X<2.7)=0.4108,$ find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ |" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290278 1072 #uuid "6a399dc0-bc45-559e-a9f3-72a30ed1d856" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290278 1074 "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.7<X<2.7) & =0.4108 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2.7) & =0.4108 \\\\ P(0<X<2.7) & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7)-P(X<0) & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7)-0.5 & =0.2054 \\\\ P(X<2.7) & =0.7054 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.7) & =0.7054\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.7) & =0.7054 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.7-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7054 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2.7}{\\sigma }) & =0.7054 \\\\ \\frac{2.7}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7054)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.54)=0.7054\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7054)=0.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.7}{\\sigma } & =0.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2.7}{0.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290205 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290279 1069 "q-21068" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290279 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(16,8)$\n- $(8,2)$\n- $(-4,4)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290279 1072 #uuid "9622b4aa-6606-5dbb-91d5-63f920f6e03c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290279 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$ \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(16,8)$, $(8,4)$ and $(-4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290231 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290280 1069 "q-21072" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290280 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290280 1072 #uuid "14df7ed3-c243-590c-b10b-e1904947b459" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290280 1074 "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289792 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290281 1069 "q-210742" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290281 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290281 1072 #uuid "944eb4d2-a6ad-5507-ae41-f7e9e113c32b" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290281 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}\\cdot  \\\\  & =6\\int u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+C \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290025 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290282 1069 "q-210746" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290282 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290282 1072 #uuid "34eec7d2-9fe5-5118-909a-f712fdea4170" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290282 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289844 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290283 1069 "q-210752" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290283 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290283 1072 #uuid "07ddd93b-2266-558f-9ab3-369202ae0194" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290283 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{2}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290000 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290284 1069 "q-210769" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290284 1071 "If the distance between the points $(b-2,2b)$ and $(1,-1)$ is minimized, then $b=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290284 1072 #uuid "6f113d04-8735-5974-90c6-4d33ae43ead3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290284 1074 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & =(b-2-1)^{2}+{(2b-(-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={b}^{2}-6b+9+4{b}^{2}+4b+1 \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-2b+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =10b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-2 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})=10>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289928 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290285 1069 "q-210773" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290285 1071 "If the distance between the points $(3a-4,a+2)$ and $(-2,6)$ is minimized, then $a=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290285 1072 #uuid "e6097e0b-0323-55bb-85aa-6c1aebe888e3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290285 1074 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(3a-4-(-2))}^{2}+{(a+2-6)}^{2} \\\\  & =(3a-2)^{2}+(a-4)^{2} \\\\  & =9{a}^{2}-12a+4+{a}^{2}-8a+16 \\\\  & =10{a}^{2}-20a+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =20a-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 20a-20 & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(1)=20>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=1.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289964 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290286 1069 "q-210779" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290286 1071 "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(a,2a-1)$ and $(4,-2),$ where $a$ is a real number?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290286 1072 #uuid "9ae50ba9-b72d-560f-8e00-a86ce7d78e7c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290286 1074 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & ={(a-4)}^{2}+{(2a-1-(-2))}^{2} \\\\  & =(a-4)^{2}+(2a+1)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-8a+16+4{a}^{2}+4a+1 \\\\  & =5{a}^{2}-4a+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =10a-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 10a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{2}{5})=10>0.$ So, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=\\frac{2}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(a)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $a=\\frac{2}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{2}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}-4(\\frac{2}{5})+17} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290079 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290287 1069 "q-210781" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290287 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290287 1072 #uuid "550df46c-f0bc-5517-a411-d24ef095d3fc" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290287 1074 "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡(2x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{{log}_{3}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{log}_{3}⁡(2x)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290099 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290288 1069 "q-211191" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290288 1071 "![](images/4e/4e343d2884532c006e83cd4c90e70a6cd9af9247194c8a7f8832ef8cbaf293b5.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290288 1072 #uuid "5031c299-7002-5210-9ac6-cb744b05632c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290288 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-8x+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-8 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=2.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289794 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290289 1069 "q-211194" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290289 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2$ that is closest to the point $(3,2).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290289 1072 #uuid "0e8dbfeb-f356-5014-87e7-43786a605372" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290289 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{x-2}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+x-2 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-5x+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(\\frac{5}{2})=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289756 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290290 1069 "q-211282" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290290 1071 "What is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2$ and the point $P(5,2)?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290290 1072 #uuid "70d7053b-bbdd-5456-80ff-2953b5e960e3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290290 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(5,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+2-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-8 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(4)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16-32+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290182 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290291 1069 "q-211288" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290291 1071 "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290291 1072 #uuid "5e2903fa-7395-523d-a538-0dfcbb4ba792" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290291 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289947 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290292 1069 "q-211303" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290292 1071 "![](images/ff/ff2956aa2885a0abddd614a11e632d3dfd1114391a9d7e27d790746c159e7f79.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290292 1072 #uuid "bba06ed1-401d-5f06-b7c3-1e90cd37f0df" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290292 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis,\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-3f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3\\cdot (-1))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-1\\cdot (-3))=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3-1)=(4,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1$ is $(4,2).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289751 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290293 1069 "q-211462" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290293 1071 "![](images/80/8000655c1943c60282d8844a2ae965b6415e21962c44309cd68806f04744b806.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290293 1072 #uuid "c0430c7a-d01d-554d-86e5-a8ca9cccf773" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290293 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis,\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $3$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **maximum** of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(2x)+3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2,3)=(1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 3)=(1,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $3$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-3+3)=(1,0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3$ is $(1,0).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289865 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290294 1069 "q-211465" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290294 1071 "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of \n$y=-f(x-1)-2?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290294 1072 #uuid "064518a2-d16c-5c0a-a491-772f5bec85b3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290294 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the right, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $2$ units down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **minimum** of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-1)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+1,3)=(3,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1\\cdot 3)=(3,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-3-2)=(3,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ is $(3,-5).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290071 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290295 1069 "q-211466" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290295 1071 "![](images/2d/2d046da9a89fe09f09dbd5e0658422ab8ccee8db680a2955ebe1ff0f361b5c35.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of \n$y=-4f(3x)?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290295 1072 #uuid "3b60ced4-eb21-5887-aa63-3d04549eb22d" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290295 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=-4f(3x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-4f(3x).$ So, we need to find the image of $(3,1),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-4f(3x),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(3,1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,4\\cdot 1)=(1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 4)=(1,-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-4f(3x)$ is $(1,-4).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289993 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290296 1069 "q-211467" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290296 1071 "![](images/d8/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290296 1072 #uuid "633e6d77-b78e-5137-95dd-2fdc7f61a086" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290296 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the left, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(1,-4),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+2)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(1,-4).$\n\n- Then, we translate the graph by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1-2,-4)=(-1,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-1\\cdot (-4))=(-1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,4+4)=(-1,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ is $(-1,8).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290189 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290297 1069 "q-211989" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290297 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int {x}^{2}{4}^{{x}^{3}/5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290297 1072 #uuid "75248609-ed60-597e-bc9d-cd586fbf6b38" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290297 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{5}.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{5}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{4}^{{x}^{3}/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {4}^{{x}^{3}/5}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {4}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int {4}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{u}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{3}/5}}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289790 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290298 1069 "q-212206" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290298 1071 "Let $a=⟨1,2,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-3,x⟩.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290298 1072 #uuid "a84da237-d454-5c5c-975c-b8671954490c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290298 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 3x-4 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289787 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290299 1069 "q-212456" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290299 1071 "Let $a=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,x⟩.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290299 1072 #uuid "c2406c43-9c5a-5fd8-81c5-a3904b70f2d3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290299 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot x \\\\  & =x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290085 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290300 1069 "q-212457" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290300 1071 "Let $a=⟨0,1,-5⟩$ and $b=⟨6,x,-2⟩.$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290300 1072 #uuid "ff2e4ed3-b952-5f49-9d70-f52c7f12daca" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290300 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 6+1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ x+10 & =0 \\\\ x & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289966 13194139533408 17592186045585 t