  & =2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290046 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290303 1069 "q-212462" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290303 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290303 1072 #uuid "6c452b5c-8f3c-5759-a6a2-00c200b73978" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290303 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot x \\\\  & =2x-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290063 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290304 1069 "q-21339" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290304 1071 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290304 1072 #uuid "c6e26edb-f404-57aa-9e8d-fef8eb7ecc78" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290304 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289867 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290305 1069 "q-21347" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290305 1071 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\nfrom $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290305 1072 #uuid "97f4a57c-d78d-5eaa-81d0-22dc3ac7dd99" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290305 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289771 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290306 1069 "q-21354" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290306 1071 "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290306 1072 #uuid "1524414a-eeb0-5a99-93da-15745003f6ea" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290306 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290160 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290307 1069 "q-21467" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290307 1071 "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a local maximum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local maximum" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290307 1072 #uuid "0d001b69-269c-523e-bfae-f0f86907fed7" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290307 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290001 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290308 1069 "q-21468" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290308 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290308 1072 #uuid "a70a3cbd-ee1f-54d1-83af-a484013d0e65" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290308 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289750 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289969 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290242 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1069 "q-214697" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $-4\\le -3x-7<2.$\n\n$-3$\n{{field-1}}\n$x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1072 #uuid "d64aa02c-7eaa-50bd-83f5-76dabde74d4a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290309 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & \\le -3x-7<2 \\\\ -4+7 & \\le -3x-7+7<2+7 \\\\ 3 & \\le -3x<9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $-3.$ Note that since we are dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & \\le -3x<9 \\\\ \\frac{3}{-3} & \\ge \\frac{-3x}{-3}>\\frac{9}{-3} \\\\ -1 & \\ge x>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution can be written as $-1\\ge x>-3$, but this is somewhat difficult to read because the larger number is on the left rather than the right. So, we will rewrite the solution by reversing the order of the numbers: \n\n$$\n-3<x\\le -1\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289805 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289911 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289959 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1069 "q-214698" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1071 "Find the solution to the inequality $1<\\frac{x-2}{3}<2.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$\n{{field-3}}\n$8$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1072 #uuid "b7793e42-3bb3-51bd-b34d-055463f56fd4" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290310 1074 "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we multiply by $3$ to eliminate the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & <\\frac{x-2}{3}<2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 1 & <3\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{3}<3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & <x-2<6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue isolating $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & <x-2<6 \\\\ 3+2 & <x-2+2<6+2 \\\\ 5 & <x<8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $5<x<8.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289797 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290032 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290176 13194139533408 1759218