nd{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 8t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2t \\\\ 2+8t \\\\ 1+t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6-2t \\\\ y=2+8t \\\\ z=1+t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290073 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290190 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1069 "q-236991" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1071 "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(2,1,0)$ and $Q(3,2,-1).$\n\n| $x=$ | $2+t$ | $t\\in [$ {{field-1}} $,1]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| $z=$ | $-t$ |  |" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1072 #uuid "19ad35f5-cc85-5c1f-ac19-ecf69dd49bdd" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290490 1074 "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =