tion vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4+2t \\\\ 1+4t \\\\ -1+2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290194 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290492 1069 "q-237014" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290492 1071 "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290492 1072 #uuid "a5d78299-26e4-51e8-b76f-fbcf41be9fda" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290492 1074 "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 15+0\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+5\\cdot x \\\\  & =5x-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 5x-30 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =30 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289752 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290493 1069 "q-237015" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290493 1071 "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290493 1072 #uuid "f879853f-8faf-5226-ac51-81b9eb8102b2" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290493 1074 "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4+x\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =14-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 14-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =14 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289848 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290494 1069 "q-237016" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290494 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290494 1072 #uuid "7a6e6afc-4f8e-5725-a54c-691ecc02b5ca" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290494 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot x+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289834 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290495 1069 "q-237017" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290495 1071 "Let $p=i-3j$ and $q=xi+2j+k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290495 1072 #uuid "9ede3719-f0d6-5ce0-9786-3cb5498e6270" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290495 1074 "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289763 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290496 1069 "q-237018" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290496 1071 "Let $a=5i-4j+3k$ and $b=-7i+xj+k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290496 1072 #uuid "23ed6766-8e64-5c6e-b8a5-b8f25dcf697a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290496 1074 "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-7)+(-4)\\cdot x+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -4x-32 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-32 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290262 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290497 1069 "q-237020" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290497 1071 "Let $p=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $q=⟨3,0,x⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290497 1072 #uuid "8158669b-62f4-54f1-8025-20269eb7a332" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290497 1074 "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 3x+6 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289778 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290498 1069 "q-237021" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290498 1071 "Let $c=⟨-3,\\sqrt{3},1⟩$ and $d=⟨x,0,-12⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290498 1072 #uuid "f85820f0-0e97-5a70-b11e-7b663b24839a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290498 1074 "Since $c$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $d$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nc⊥d\\,⟹\\,c\\cdot d=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot x+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289812 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290499 1069 "q-237022" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290499 1071 "Let $p=⟨3,5,x⟩$ and $q=⟨-3,1,2⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290499 1072 #uuid "487960be-d7be-5dc4-ae5e-e966e5d0db00" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290499 1074 "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 1+x\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290023 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290500 1069 "q-237031" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290500 1071 "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to $y=4x-6$ that passes through the point $(8,-3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290500 1072 #uuid "9140d2e6-111a-5292-b6b0-fffff303bdec" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290500 1074 "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=4x-6,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(8,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290213 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290501 1069 "q-237036" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290501 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-5,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290501 1072 #uuid "8c855e16-f691-5fbd-bcf1-319b489f96c6" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290501 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-3)}{2-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-5,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x+5) \\\\ y-5 & =-x-5 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290130 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290502 1069 "q-237193" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290502 1071 "![](images/5a/5add9f877dc2e93b1c8e562794aa64c83d180f5c683735055ee24f8a190e8740.png)\n\nThe equation of the line ${l}_{1}$ is $y=-4x-3$ and ${l}_{2}$ is perpendicular to ${l}_{1}.$  What is the equation of ${l}_{2}?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290502 1072 #uuid "383ada0a-1bdb-5618-9cd6-7908256a85cd" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290502 1074 "Since perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $-4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-4) \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{1}{4}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289944 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290503 1069 "q-237197" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290503 1071 "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2$ and $y=mx+b$ are perpendicular. If the line $y=mx+b$ passes through the point $(6,-1),$ what is the value of $b?$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290503 1072 #uuid "3a5691d5-2f92-5293-81b7-0db83b2ca8e7" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290503 1074 "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-2,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $\\frac{2}{5},$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(6,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-6) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+15 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=14.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289921 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290504 1069 "q-237224" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290504 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290504 1072 #uuid "7172ccc4-2a2a-5ae6-a89d-55424e3bf138" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290504 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6}{5}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6}{5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289943 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290505 1069 "q-237225" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290505 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290505 1072 #uuid "01d34255-de74-5cbb-862e-52884bec6cfd" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290505 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-3)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =-\\frac{2}{7}(x-0) \\\\ y+5 & =-\\frac{2}{7}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{7}x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289956 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290506 1069 "q-237230" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290506 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,3)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290506 1072 #uuid "a0b26a33-738b-5140-8f04-e3933793a909" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290506 1074 "First, we write the equation $5x+y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-5x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-5x+2.$ The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+4) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289737 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290507 1069 "q-237231" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290507 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,0)$ and is perpendicular to $x-6y=-3?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290507 1072 #uuid "515eea7d-54c4-5f7a-8f42-e28d3350452e" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290507 1074 "First, we write the equation $x-6y=-3$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6y & =-3 \\\\ -6y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-6.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-6$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-6(x-(-7)) \\\\ y & =-6(x+7) \\\\ y & =-6x-42\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289755 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290508 1069 "q-237232" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290508 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,1)$ and is perpendicular to $4x-3y=7?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290508 1072 #uuid "bd2e4750-a1c2-5012-bd49-32f6921fcef3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290508 1074 "First, we write the equation $4x-3y=7$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y & =7 \\\\ -3y & =-4x+7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{4}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290215 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290509 1069 "q-237438" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290509 1071 "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=0$ and ${a}_{2}=3,$ then ${a}_{3}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290509 1072 #uuid "7745591b-7fec-5163-891b-ca6f7e18835f" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290509 1074 "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3+0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290254 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290510 1069 "q-2379" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290510 1071 "$\\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290510 1072 #uuid "46e8f3d6-924e-554c-9c9c-a93b6ef8fe0f" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290510 1074 "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $(3-{x}^{2}).$ But the derivative is $-2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor. \n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=3-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\,⟹\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {(3-{x}^{2})}^{5}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}{u}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}{(3-{x}^{2})}^{6}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{(3-{x}^{2})}^{6}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290151 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290228 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1069 "q-237946" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1071 "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1072 #uuid "fa483285-7fb1-55f0-9e07-a601fc5a4d37" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290511 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3x}-9\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-9)\\cdot {5}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-9=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,3.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289815 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290512 1069 "q-23796" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290512 1071 "${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290512 1072 #uuid "bc8dd438-a2fc-517c-b7e9-e06f0f739289" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290512 1074 "First, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the parentheses and use the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }-1 \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289736 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290513 1069 "q-23799" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290513 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290513 1072 #uuid "a183fbf8-0afb-55d8-9c23-9f82dba8cb3e" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290513 1074 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta $ using its definition and simplify further:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289974 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290514 1069 "q-2380" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290514 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290514 1072 #uuid "f9ed1d83-c155-582f-82be-1fc779359a21" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290514 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289754 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290515 1069 "q-2382" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290515 1071 "$\\int z{e}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290515 1072 #uuid "8c2e05bf-d6ab-5dd9-a54b-6f33a76c6868" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290515 1074 "Let's introduce the new variable $u={z}^{2}.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=2z\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=z\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {e}^{{z}^{2}}\\cdot z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{2}}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289954 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290516 1069 "q-23847" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290516 1071 "$({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290516 1072 #uuid "d2b71b00-c11b-5c0b-9ab2-a575a74118da" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290516 1074 "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nAlso, recall the Pythagorean formula for sines and cosines:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above identities, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+{tan}^{2}⁡x & =1+{tan}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290096 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290517 1069 "q-23849" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290517 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290517 1072 #uuid "0a349287-5d4c-537c-9bb0-00fd8c054f79" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290517 1074 "First, we operate the first two terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{({cot}^{2}⁡x-{csc}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{-({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-({csc}^{2}⁡x-{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{-1}{{csc}^{2}⁡x}+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290140 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290518 1069 "q-2385" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290518 1071 "If $f(x)=\\frac{cos⁡x}{10{x}^{2}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290518 1072 #uuid "3b0fa11c-feb9-540f-a74b-38792fbe2335" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290518 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=cos⁡x$ and $v(x)=10{x}^{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-sin⁡x)(10{x}^{2})-(cos⁡x)(20x)}{(10{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10{x}^{2}sin⁡x-20xcos⁡x}{100{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{10x}(-xsin⁡x-2cos⁡x)}{\\cancel{10x}\\cdot 10{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-xsin⁡x-2cos⁡x}{10{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289785 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290519 1069 "q-23853" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290519 1071 "Simplify ${sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290519 1072 #uuid "a01be135-3059-50f3-96f4-e6908bb8d8f2" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290519 1074 "First, we can simplify our expression by factoring it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+({tan}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sec}^{4}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡x+{tan}^{4}⁡x=1.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289994 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290520 1069 "q-2388" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290520 1071 "Calculate $\\int x({x}^{2}-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290520 1072 #uuid "c383c96e-d00b-5142-a0f8-5447857bcdf7" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290520 1074 "In this case, the $x$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{2}-1).$ But the derivative is $2x,$ so we're only out by a constant factor.\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x({x}^{2}-1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-1)^{3}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({x}^{2}-1)^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)^{4}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289950 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290521 1069 "q-23896" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290521 1071 "Simplify ${sec}^{2}⁡x({cot}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290521 1072 #uuid "e817b7ff-13b9-5c41-abb9-36ddedcd3f8b" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290521 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses using the distributive property. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x({cot}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x({cot}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x) & ={cot}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289761 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290522 1069 "q-23897" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290522 1071 "Simplify $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290522 1072 #uuid "d2d6dd75-f063-5feb-ad58-0c61bba9a5c9" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290522 1074 "First, we use the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =[1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ]+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor out ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ and we use the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta $ again. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\, \\\\  & ={csc}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289793 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290523 1069 "q-23898" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290523 1071 "Simplify ${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290523 1072 #uuid "2ce8a935-1da8-5328-91b9-35c9544cd048" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290523 1074 "First, we write the given expression as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor out ${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and use the identity \n${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290028 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290524 1069 "q-2390" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290524 1071 "Calculate $\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290524 1072 #uuid "f908f52d-efaa-5ed9-92ad-fd2900015e8a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290524 1074 "We note that $10x$ is the derivative of the quantity in the square root. So we make a substitution\n\n$$\nu=5{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =10x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=10x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand rewriting our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,10x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{(\\frac{3}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we substitute $u=5{x}^{2}-4$ back in the expression. So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\int 10x\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{2(5{x}^{2}-4)^{3/2}}{3}+C.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290027 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290525 1069 "q-2392" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290525 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={a}^{3x}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290525 1072 #uuid "d4cb91b7-6460-5845-ba44-f35610f01889" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290525 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $v(x)={a}^{3x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡x)({a}^{3x})+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(3{a}^{3x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡a) \\\\  & ={a}^{3x}({sec}^{2}⁡x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡a\\operatorname{tan}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290003 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290526 1069 "q-2397" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290526 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ when $f(x)={sin}^{3}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290526 1072 #uuid "d9cfcb56-203d-51a2-aa05-78075c70500a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290526 1074 "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,v=9{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=18x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)\\cdot 18x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1))^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1))\\cdot 18x \\\\  & =54x{sin}^{2}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9{x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289743 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290527 1069 "q-2401" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290527 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290527 1072 #uuid "f5501845-7745-5f41-81f4-6fab62788d36" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290527 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=4{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=8x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(8x)(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})+(4{x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}) \\\\  & =8x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290244 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290528 1069 "q-2403" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290528 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290528 1072 #uuid "c3d7d091-4a85-557a-b384-1cb4715f620a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290528 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}})(2x)-(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x})(2)}{(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289854 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290256 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1069 "q-240607" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1071 "![](images/a3/a3527c741af26b2135b2d676be4f055c489cd143ec7af645445219633de9645d.png)\n\nWrite the equation of the circle given above in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}} $=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1072 #uuid "b2e57ee6-00a1-5952-859b-e2b4b55908a9" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290529 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and has radius $r=1.$ So, the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={1}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289779 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290530 1069 "q-240666" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290530 1071 "![](images/d4/d4c21d6ce09ff32004a28d18d223fef7858c377bccb0eb532cf2dda29a545f5e.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290530 1072 #uuid "b992bd0f-c103-5e7d-8240-431930cfc4f1" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290530 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-7,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-11,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-7,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+2)^{2}=4p(x+7).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-11,-6),$ we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+2)^{2} & =4p(-11+7) \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =-4(x+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =-4(x+7) \\\\ 4 & =-4(x+7) \\\\ -1 & =x+7 \\\\ x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-8.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289930 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290531 1069 "q-24069" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290531 1071 "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ and ${0}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290531 1072 #uuid "f537dceb-5fdb-5fdd-9eed-d90c522b7358" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290531 1074 "![](images/9d/9d9aa1e6ffac387c53c7a1b35217a51cd17db7242ddedec4ff72c229a73d5291.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290210 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290532 1069 "q-24072" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290532 1071 "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290532 1072 #uuid "cb825d4a-21ae-55df-8bd3-4e6fa7cb77e6" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290532 1074 "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/b8/b81b4602d221eb04aab76b0ed8853ab75a5c3cf93e76cc489344bc15133c4daf.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{5})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289776 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290533 1069 "q-24074" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290533 1071 "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{13}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290533 1072 #uuid "2acc488c-8752-546a-8c14-2587df0cb445" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290533 1074 "![](images/d6/d6ec4c0b71e64cd659bceea63a49f0ef09c44ee1c31353fd377963d7cdf3c3db.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{13}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{13}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{12}{13}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289893 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290534 1069 "q-2413" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290534 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t$ is given by $v(t)=8{t}^{2}-3t.$ What is the total displacement of the particle between $t=0$ and $t=6?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290534 1072 #uuid "aff856fc-9f91-58c4-8ef3-ea3ff3dbaa0f" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290534 1074 "We find the total displacement by calculating the definite integral of the velocity.\n\nLet $d$ be the total displacement. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}(8{t}^{2}-3t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{8{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8(6)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3(6)^{2}}{2}]-[\\frac{8(0)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3(0)^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =[576-54]-0 \\\\  & =522.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the total displacement between $t=0$ and $t=6$ is $522.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290165 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290535 1069 "q-2414" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290535 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t.$ \nAt time $t=2,$ the particle is located at the position $x=6.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=5?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290535 1072 #uuid "932c2819-1901-55ca-8222-e90c97f1fee5" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290535 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(5) & =6+{\\int }_{2}^{5}(3{t}^{2}-6t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =6+{[{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}]}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =6+([(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}]-[(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}]) \\\\  & =6+([125-75]-[8-12]) \\\\  & =6+(50-(-4)) \\\\  & =6+54 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x=60.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289872 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290536 1069 "q-2417" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290536 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{2}+4t.$ At time $t=4$ the particle is located at the position $x=-300.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290536 1072 #uuid "f85a9030-17ea-52b5-b702-caeff6c40b98" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290536 1074 "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=4$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [4,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(4)+{\\int }_{4}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(7) & =-300+{\\int }_{4}^{7}(6{t}^{2}+4t)\\,\\text{dt} \\\\  & =-300+{[2{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})]}_{4}^{7} \\\\  & =-300+([2(7)^{3}+2(7)^{2}]-[2(4)^{3}+2(4)^{2}]) \\\\  & =-300+([686+98]-[128+32]) \\\\  & =-300+(784-160) \\\\  & =-300+624 \\\\  & =324.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=324.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290067 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290537 1069 "q-2420" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290537 1071 "$\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290537 1072 #uuid "00c040ca-17ee-59e4-9387-a983daa9bae0" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290537 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{5}^{x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={5}^{x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}-\\frac{{5}^{x}}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))^{2}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290175 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290538 1069 "q-242899" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290538 1071 "Given that ${8}^{1-x}={3}^{x/2},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290538 1072 #uuid "905090eb-9e63-5fb8-8001-0c20fb8ec0d1" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290538 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{8}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{1-x} & ={3}^{x/2} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡({8}^{1-x}) & ={log}_{8}⁡({3}^{x/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n1-x={log}_{8}⁡({3}^{x/2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n1-x=\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-x & =\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3 \\\\ 1 & =x+\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3 \\\\ x+\\frac{x}{2}{log}_{8}⁡3 & =1 \\\\ 2x+x{log}_{8}⁡3 & =2 \\\\ x(2+{log}_{8}⁡3) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{2+{log}_{8}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{8}⁡(3)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}≈0.528\\,321.\n$$\n\nFinally,  we can approximate $x$ as \n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2}{2+0.528\\,321}≈0.791,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289758 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290539 1069 "q-2429" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290539 1071 "$\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290539 1072 #uuid "ca4b3b9a-6ea7-5162-ab23-944642e8e7e5" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290539 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=2\\sqrt{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(2\\sqrt{x})-\\int (2\\sqrt{x})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-4\\sqrt{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290088 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290540 1069 "q-242902" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290540 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x+2}={2}^{x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290540 1072 #uuid "f1f31343-a477-520d-bcc1-1e38225689fe" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290540 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & ={2}^{x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+2=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+2 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289798 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290541 1069 "q-242903" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290541 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{3x-1}={6}^{2x+1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290541 1072 #uuid "1285c9cd-7326-5f21-8cc4-238b71cf12c1" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290541 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x-1} & ={6}^{2x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n3x-1=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x(3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289847 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290542 1069 "q-242904" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290542 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{2x-2}=2\\cdot {9}^{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290542 1072 #uuid "1b42f4ed-83b8-53d9-a72c-341c82507fbf" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290542 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-2} & =2\\cdot {9}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {9}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {9}^{x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x}) \\\\ 2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 2x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290198 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290543 1069 "q-242906" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290543 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-2}={10}^{-3x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290543 1072 #uuid "53da998b-0a24-5c87-8aa3-14d65ca4fccd" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290543 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-2} & ={10}^{-3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({10}^{-3x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({10}^{-3x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) & =2 \\\\ x(1+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290240 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290544 1069 "q-242909" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290544 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x}={9}^{5-x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290544 1072 #uuid "09b5bb2d-aa9a-5431-90be-e4310ccd1e4a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290544 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & ={9}^{5-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{5-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{5-x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx=(5-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(5-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289938 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290545 1069 "q-242914" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290545 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${3}^{4-x}={8}^{2x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290545 1072 #uuid "d1a98c1d-e002-56dc-98cf-733b76c221c0" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290545 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-x} & ={8}^{2x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{4-x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)=(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ -x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289757 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290546 1069 "q-242918" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290546 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${8}^{2x}={10}^{x-7}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290546 1072 #uuid "dee9d0cb-7e2c-55e6-97ab-24576cae3424" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290546 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2x} & ={10}^{x-7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({10}^{x-7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)=(x-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =(x-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) & =-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290037 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290547 1069 "q-242919" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290547 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${5}^{4x+1}={6}^{7x+5}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290547 1072 #uuid "c423a638-983b-5ebe-b413-4d69284f1a8b" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290547 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{4x+1} & ={6}^{7x+5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{7x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)=(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290247 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290548 1069 "q-242922" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290548 1071 "Find the solution of ${2}^{5x-1}={7}^{3-x}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290548 1072 #uuid "7cdb8498-47d2-5daa-a38e-bb8635d282cc" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290548 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5x-1} & ={7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ 5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289933 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290549 1069 "q-242923" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290549 1071 "Find the solution of ${3}^{2x}={8}^{1-6x}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290549 1072 #uuid "399f64e6-0f82-5ca3-861d-fbe99de11ba6" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290549 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2x} & ={8}^{1-6x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{1-6x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(1-6x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8))}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290166 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290550 1069 "q-242938" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290550 1071 "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x+1}={2}^{x}?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290550 1072 #uuid "8c52d167-9370-5103-baea-7365420c35b8" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290550 1074 "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={2}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-x & =1 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290056 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290551 1069 "q-24322" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290551 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290551 1072 #uuid "2abb4fd3-0dbf-5cf1-b72d-0a77012faa09" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290551 1074 "First, we rewrite the expression using the definitions of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta $:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3(\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use $1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ to simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{3\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290246 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290552 1069 "q-24341" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290552 1071 "Express $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290552 1072 #uuid "a509f6a5-78b4-503c-a592-ded49f6e4644" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290552 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290018 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290553 1069 "q-24378" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290553 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290553 1072 #uuid "92fe000e-8b10-5f6c-a7a8-e9bb8ce2fa57" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290553 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289885 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290554 1069 "q-2439" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290554 1071 "The weight $w,$ in pounds, of a baby gorilla $t$ years after birth can be modeled by the function $w(t)=4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+15.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of his weight when $t=1$ year?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290554 1072 #uuid "89c5ed41-56cc-572f-ab21-6b6ef12532fd" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290554 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of weight when $t=1$ year is equal to ${w}^{′}(1).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+15) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =12-2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the weight when $t=1$ year is $10$ pounds per year." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290065 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290555 1069 "q-2440" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290555 1071 "A particle moves in a straight line so that after $t$ seconds it is $p(t)={t}^{3}-11t+9$ feet from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position when $t=3$ seconds?" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290555 1072 #uuid "9eeec890-ca5d-58b4-bb58-4448bce1aaf7" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290555 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position when $t=3$ seconds is equal to ${p}^{′}(3).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-11t+9) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-11 \\\\  & =27-11 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the particle's position when $t=3$ seconds is $16$ feet per second." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289888 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290556 1069 "q-24499" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290556 1071 "Express ${cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290556 1072 #uuid "ccbcdf42-d0d6-5ef1-b1a3-dd2467d078f8" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290556 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290195 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290557 1069 "q-2455" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290557 1071 "For the function $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)>0$\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290557 1072 #uuid "abc739c6-0758-56d6-9f78-098fd3207a39" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290557 1074 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=3x-{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3(1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=\\pm 1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289766 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290558 1069 "q-24574" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290558 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290558 1072 #uuid "50632a56-05a3-5b2c-a470-4387daec2008" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290558 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1 \\\\ y & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289892 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290559 1069 "q-24575" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290559 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290559 1072 #uuid "ca1f80dd-221f-5c68-b74a-e39a400a3763" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290559 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{4}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1 \\\\ y & =4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{9}-1).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290030 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290560 1069 "q-24576" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290560 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290560 1072 #uuid "fd03cb1f-af7d-59db-bf84-fa1a13cb0a30" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290560 1074 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290203 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290561 1069 "q-2458" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290561 1071 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11$ is concave down." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290561 1072 #uuid "37023784-3cf7-50eb-b6c2-80808faab416" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290561 1074 "A function $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the second derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-11:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-6x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave down, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)<0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x+4 & <0 \\\\ 6x & >4 \\\\ x & >\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290135 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290562 1069 "q-2460" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290562 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290562 1072 #uuid "ee10b456-721a-5520-9957-17e358e1429f" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290562 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=0.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =6(-1)=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =6(1)=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290053 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290563 1069 "q-2464" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290563 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290563 1072 #uuid "f0de753a-032b-5d81-9dcc-6e98f80b1272" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290563 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290142 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290564 1069 "q-24641" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290564 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290564 1072 #uuid "4eaa49cb-94d5-5c4a-8e77-4592aca20df1" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290564 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={30}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {30}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-1+{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity in the form ${sin}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}=1-{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290207 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290565 1069 "q-24645" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290565 1071 "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290565 1072 #uuid "4270d786-b50e-5319-88f8-bca386712273" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290565 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290253 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290566 1069 "q-24647" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290566 1071 "Express $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290566 1072 #uuid "feecaade-207a-51bf-96ee-61bce1de2e85" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290566 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289973 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290567 1069 "q-2465" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290567 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-6x+7.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290567 1072 #uuid "40cde6a2-3451-52ec-aa86-ea24a0db6b71" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290567 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+12x-6 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n12x+12=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)+12=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290129 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290568 1069 "q-24650" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290568 1071 "$8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290568 1072 #uuid "f30309d1-f3e3-55ac-be8a-acbe0c0f980a" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290568 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}) & =4\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289951 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290569 1069 "q-246716" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290569 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of the following data set:\n\n$$\n10,\\,23,\\,17,\\,18\n$$\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290569 1072 #uuid "07d9c62b-675c-5d1b-b898-12192ba53627" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290569 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=4$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (10+23+17+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 68 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(10-17)^{2}+(23-17)^{2}+(17-17)^{2}+(18-17)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[49+36+0+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{86}{4} \\\\  & =21.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290230 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290570 1069 "q-246721" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290570 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n12,\\,15,\\,15,\\,17,\\,21,\\,22\n$$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290570 1072 #uuid "a60b9f00-88b9-5158-9c75-cda002d864da" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290570 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (12+15+15+17+21+22) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot ({12}^{2}+{15}^{2}+{15}^{2}+{17}^{2}+{21}^{2}+{22}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (144+225+225+289+441+484) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 1808 \\\\  & =\\frac{1808}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{904}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{904}{3}-{(17)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{904}{3}-289 \\\\  & =\\frac{904-867}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290206 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290571 1069 "q-246758" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290571 1071 "Given that $P(A)=0.4,$ $P(B)=0.5,$ and $P(A\\cap B)=0.2,$ find $P(A\\cap {B}^{′}).$\n\n$P(A\\cap {B}^{′})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290571 1072 #uuid "3683ca08-80a0-57b2-aedc-0937ab35c229" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290571 1074 "First, we calculate $P(A\\cup B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\  & =0.4+0.5-0.2 \\\\  & =0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the corresponding Venn diagram and shade the region $A\\cap {B}^{′}.$\n\n![](images/52/52a7c0ed9285d2699087f0ef817257eacebfabd5cfb2117167730a66b94da3e6.png)\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap {B}^{′})=0.2.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289990 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290572 1069 "q-2469" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290572 1071 "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290572 1072 #uuid "54523433-2b3d-5350-9453-344ec229fc6f" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290572 1074 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290058 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290573 1069 "q-2471" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290573 1071 "For the function $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-2)<0$\n- $x=-2$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290573 1072 #uuid "19694b62-f531-5e43-9d81-ba1b9a788be0" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290573 1074 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+15{x}^{2}-10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=15{x}^{2}+30x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{x}^{2}+30x & =0 \\\\ 15x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,-2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=30x+30,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =30>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =-30<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I is true while statement II is false. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is true while statement VI is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-2,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290081 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290574 1069 "q-2472" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290574 1071 "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for  $f(x)=2x$ on the interval $[1,3].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290574 1072 #uuid "8f0edaee-3cfb-50c7-84ca-c9bc566266d1" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290574 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({x}^{2})|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =4 \\\\ 2c & =4 \\\\ c & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289868 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290575 1069 "q-2474" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290575 1071 "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=-x+2$ on the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290575 1072 #uuid "1b81302d-a9bb-5a10-99b4-f89c6bcb3c20" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290575 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals. we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-(-2)}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(-x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x)|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2\\cdot 2)-(-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}+2\\cdot (-2))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[2-(-6)] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =2 \\\\ -c+2 & =2 \\\\ c & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289817 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290576 1069 "q-24756" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290576 1071 "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290576 1072 #uuid "3afdea11-e4df-5ff6-be80-8dd151f3d450" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290576 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={30}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289977 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290577 1069 "q-24757" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290577 1071 "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290577 1072 #uuid "a710f83e-0182-5b44-96f0-5ba7ee68c68b" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290577 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289735 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290578 1069 "q-2476" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290578 1071 "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ on the interval $[1,2].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290578 1072 #uuid "f00f62ea-0e54-554f-9b3a-424cd124b9db" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290578 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}})-(4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{4}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =\\frac{19}{4} \\\\ 4+\\frac{2}{{c}^{3}} & =\\frac{19}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{c}^{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{c}^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}^{3} & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{3}} \\\\ c & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290149 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290579 1069 "q-24763" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290579 1071 "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290579 1072 #uuid "71348998-c87f-57f5-9c8c-a546aec4acc5" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290579 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289830 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290580 1069 "q-24764" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290580 1071 "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290580 1072 #uuid "6dfab8f9-1856-571a-9716-248451182789" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290580 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289782 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290581 1069 "q-2477" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290581 1071 "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-2$ on the interval $[1,3].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290581 1072 #uuid "5202402f-5aab-522d-9cf5-edc4e7890d11" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290581 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(3{x}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({x}^{3}-2x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[({3}^{3}-2\\cdot 3)-({1}^{3}-2\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(21+1) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =11 \\\\ 3{c}^{2}-2 & =11 \\\\ 3{c}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ c & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $c\\in [1,3],$ we take only $c=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{3}}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290114 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290582 1069 "q-24781" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290582 1071 "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290582 1072 #uuid "c8b170de-4305-57f7-8f86-f2f06478c4e5" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290582 1074 "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290232 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290583 1069 "q-24792" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290583 1071 "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290583 1072 #uuid "87e7afa8-1798-5127-a9ff-a08924db07de" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290583 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289791 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290584 1069 "q-2483" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290584 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot arccotx.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290584 1072 #uuid "0452209a-6bf5-5ff9-86d8-fd513bd202d6" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290584 1074 "The formula for the derivative of $arccot$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot arccotx) \\\\  & =arccotx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccotx) \\\\  & =arccotx\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{arccotx}{x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289852 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290585 1069 "q-2484" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290585 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=arccscx\\cdot arcsecx$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290585 1072 #uuid "fabb0dc5-fadf-5950-bf52-26606893c541" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290585 1074 "The formulae for the derivatives of $arccsc$ and $arcsec$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccscx) & =-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsecx) & =\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formulae above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =arcsecx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arccscx)+arccscx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(arcsecx) \\\\  & =arcsecx\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}})+arccscx\\cdot (\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{arcsecx}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+\\frac{arccscx}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{arccscx-arcsecx}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290164 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290586 1069 "q-2490" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290586 1071 "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=2x$ on the interval $[-1,4].$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290586 1072 #uuid "c90233bc-1ba5-5e36-a9ed-4dc2329080e9" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290586 1074 "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-(-1)}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ({x}^{2})|}_{-1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[{4}^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(16-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =3 \\\\ 2c & =3 \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289781 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289896 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290238 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1069 "q-249695" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1071 "The circle with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=196$ is centered at $(${{field-1}}$,$ \n{{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1072 #uuid "05bd96d3-1d86-5f40-b7a4-9cde34b0832b" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290587 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=196$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =196 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{196} \\\\ r & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $r=14.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290082 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290097 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290143 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1069 "q-249699" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1071 "The circle with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=11$ is centered at $(${{field-1}}$,$ \n{{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1072 #uuid "7a7f82eb-1ef4-5978-be34-7e66b42dd7d8" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290588 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=11$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =11 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $r=\\sqrt{11}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289765 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290086 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1069 "q-249857" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1071 "![](images/4f/4ff922cede2568837a30dedfcfdbd5e956eb5a1df7d099ad031bfbddfbb7f00b.png)\n\nWrite the equation of the circle given above in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}} $=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1072 #uuid "21eefa62-6907-5ba3-b2f5-017f87bccca9" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290589 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and has radius $r=3.$ So, the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289839 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289980 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1069 "q-249864" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1071 "Construct an equation that describes the set of all points $(x,y)$ that are a distance of $25$ units from the origin. Express your answer in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1072 #uuid "11007c2f-0c7d-55e8-a70a-68adac5c7664" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290590 1074 "The set of all points $(x,y)$ that are a distance of $25$ units from the origin defines a circle with radius $r=25$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d526d47989c735bcdd8629b76246c3bd88d4b3a142bcdffb33ea8bc0c51cb31e.png)\n\nSo, the required equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={25}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =625.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289996 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290591 1069 "q-2501" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290591 1071 "$\\int \\frac{2}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290591 1072 #uuid "0be79b52-86d6-59fc-99d7-9763abd0657c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290591 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{arcsec}\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\text{ arcsec }y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290235 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290592 1069 "q-2503" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290592 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290592 1072 #uuid "2b7a5d31-b8c0-5e03-b3eb-62f1624001b3" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290592 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/9)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$  \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289870 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289940 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1069 "q-250392" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+14x+49)+1=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1072 #uuid "a5d4b894-8370-5aa0-9969-b46213d35ba2" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290593 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+14x+49 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+14x+49)+1 & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2}+1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289914 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290044 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1069 "q-250395" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+12x+{6}^{2})+4=($\n{{field-1}}${)}^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1072 #uuid "3eba8d63-fcde-55e4-8de8-74060f0333c0" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290594 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}={(x+a)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x+6)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+12x+{6}^{2})+4 & = \\\\ {(\\,x+6\\,)}^{2}+4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289958 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290159 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1069 "q-250396" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({y}^{2}+20y+{10}^{2})+7=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1072 #uuid "9ae1663e-9a5d-554f-a1c8-d529dc76a818" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290595 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+20y+{10}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+10{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+20y+{10}^{2})+7 & = \\\\ (y+10{)}^{2}+7 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289873 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290223 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1069 "q-250397" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+4x+4)-3=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1072 #uuid "c4bff1c6-d900-5e76-a075-002e3234a55b" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290596 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+4 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+4x+4)-3 & = \\\\ (x+2{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289939 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290597 1069 "q-2504" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290597 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290597 1072 #uuid "f3817a61-9f5a-501b-a646-24bc46868497" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290597 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $16{x}^{2}=(4x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=4x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(4x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289775 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289809 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1069 "q-250428" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-18x+81)+2=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1072 #uuid "173d7c13-482b-5042-aa02-eb3bdb23355c" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290598 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-18x+81 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 9x+{9}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-9{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-18x+81)+2 & = \\\\ (x-9{)}^{2}+2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289920 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289962 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1069 "q-250452" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+22y+131.$\n\n${y}^{2}+22y+131=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1072 #uuid "b5119667-6fd3-5c0b-a869-38b5d4e22ecc" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290599 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+131\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${11}^{2},$ writing the constant term $131$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 11y+{11}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+11{)}^{2}-{11}^{2}+131\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+11{)}^{2}+10.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289981 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290187 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1069 "q-250454" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+8y+13.$\n\n${y}^{2}+8y+13=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1072 #uuid "db840383-bcd3-5f5d-9b3d-d199d009ebf4" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290600 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+13\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $13$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+4{)}^{2}-{4}^{2}+13\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+4{)}^{2}-3.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290131 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290249 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1069 "q-250465" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-24y+44.$\n\n${y}^{2}-24y+44=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1072 #uuid "14bbb855-11da-520e-8a63-9ebbe8ba5c80" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290601 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+44\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2},$ writing the constant term $44$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{12}^{2}+44\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-12{)}^{2}-{12}^{2}+44\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-12{)}^{2}-100.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289926 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290042 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1069 "q-250466" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-10y+53.$\n\n${y}^{2}-10y+53=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1072 #uuid "aaadd0e7-cf4f-5bca-a981-28baacc544d2" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290602 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+53\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $53$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+53\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+28.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290075 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290201 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1069 "q-250517" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1071 "If the expression ${x}^{2}-10x$ is written as $(x-p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1072 #uuid "c1f54e2d-7c34-56b8-9fdc-fc34e2e62fea" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290603 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289935 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290015 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1069 "q-250575" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1071 "Solve the equation $(x-6)^{2}=-9.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1072 #uuid "f2029dbf-5789-5f4a-819d-abe0e31d1520" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290604 1074 "The left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x-6 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-9} \\\\ x-6 & =\\pm 3\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =6\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=6+3\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=6-3\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290146 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290605 1069 "q-2506" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290605 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290605 1072 #uuid "125b10d6-12e0-5b91-bec6-9d12798feef7" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290605 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(3x)}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289922 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290606 1069 "q-2507" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290606 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290606 1072 #uuid "d025ad08-0459-5553-af22-02ed776a0b57" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290606 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}=(5x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289960 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290607 1069 "q-250824" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290607 1071 "If the sum of the first $20$ terms of an arithmetic series is ${S}_{20}=-635,$ and the last term is ${a}_{20}=-65,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290607 1072 #uuid "7f08431f-0552-50f5-8547-3b66cd2de7ef" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290607 1074 "We are given the last term of our series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{20}=-65$, $N=20$, and ${S}_{N}=-635$ into the formula above and solving for ${a}_{1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-635 & =\\frac{20}{2}({a}_{1}+(-65)) \\\\ -635 & =10({a}_{1}-65) \\\\ -\\frac{635}{10} & ={a}_{1}-65 \\\\ -63.5 & ={a}_{1}-65 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289786 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290608 1069 "q-250826" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290608 1071 "If the sum of the first $32$ terms of an arithmetic series is ${S}_{32}=648,$ and the last term is ${a}_{32}=37.3,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290608 1072 #uuid "895a010d-8c8d-5a5a-9b5e-9b62bc3f8757" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290608 1074 "We are given the last term of our series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{32}=37.3$, $N=32$, and ${S}_{N}=648$ into the formula above and solving for ${a}_{1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}648 & =\\frac{32}{2}({a}_{1}+37.3) \\\\ 648 & =16({a}_{1}+37.3) \\\\ \\frac{648}{16} & ={a}_{1}+37.3 \\\\ 40.5 & ={a}_{1}+37.3 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290156 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290609 1069 "q-250834" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290609 1071 "If the sum of the first $36$ terms of an arithmetic series is ${S}_{36}=2\\,142,$ and the common difference is $d=3,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290609 1072 #uuid "5232e233-11ce-5856-a4ca-bc44cbc41838" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290609 1074 "We are given the common difference of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=36$, $d=3$, and ${S}_{N}=2\\,142$ into the formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,142 & =\\frac{36}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(36-1)(3)) \\\\ 2\\,142 & =18(2{a}_{1}+105) \\\\ 119 & =2{a}_{1}+105 \\\\ 14 & =2{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =7.\\end{align