{1}{5}) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & ={a}_{1}+\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290014 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290612 1069 "q-250878" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290612 1071 "If the $9$th term of an arithmetic series is $\\frac{46}{3}$ and the sum of the first $15$ terms is $200,$ then the first term of the series is ${a}_{1}=${{field-1}}." 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290612 1072 #uuid "c3203d00-54ac-567f-8b42-d9e1e88f86e6" 13194139533408 17592186045585 true] [17592186290612 1074 "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=15$ and ${S}_{N}=200$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{15}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(15-1)d) \\\\ 400 & =15(2{a}_{1}+14d) \\\\ \\frac{400}{15} & =2{a}_{1}+14d \\\\ \\frac{80}{3} & =2{a}_{1}+14d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $9$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{9} & ={a}_{1}+(9-1)d \\\\ \\frac{46}{3} & ={a}_{1}+8d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{46}{3}={a}_{1}+8d \\\\ \\frac{80}{3}=2{a}_{1}+14d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{92}{3} & =-2{a}_{1}-16d \\\\ \\frac{80}{3} & =2{a}_{1}+14d \\\\ -\\frac{12}{3} & =-2d \\\\ d & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substitutin